.TH SSH 2
.SH NAME
ssh: connect, login, run, Conn, writepkt, readpkt \- SSH-2 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "security.m";
include "encoding.m";
include "asn1.m";
include "pkcs.m";
include "ssh.m";
ssh := load Ssh Ssh->PATH;

init:           fn();
connect:        fn(addr, host, wantthumb: string): (ref Conn, string);
rsakey_pem:     fn(pem: string): (ref Authkey, string);
rsakey_factotum: fn(keyspec: string): (ref Authkey, string);
login:      fn(c: ref Conn, user: string, key: ref Authkey,
                password: string): string;
run:        fn(c: ref Conn, cmd: string): (ref Result, string);
shell:      fn(c: ref Conn, usepty: int, term: string, cols, rows: int,
                in, out, err: ref Sys->FD): (int, string);

Conn: adt {
        fd:        ref Sys->FD;
        vc:        string;
        vs:        string;
        encrypted: int;
        sid:       array of byte;
        hostkey:   array of byte;
        thumb:     string;

        readpkt:  fn(c: self ref Conn): (array of byte, string);
        writepkt: fn(c: self ref Conn, payload: array of byte): string;
        close:    fn(c: self ref Conn);
};
Authkey: adt { blob: array of byte; n, d: ref IPint; modlen: int; };
Result:  adt { stdout, stderr: array of byte; status: int; };

MSG_KEXINIT, MSG_NEWKEYS, MSG_SERVICE_REQUEST,
MSG_SERVICE_ACCEPT, MSG_USERAUTH_REQUEST, ...: con ...;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ssh
is an SSH-2 transport-and-connection client written in Limbo, ported
from 9front's
.I /sys/src/cmd/ssh.c
and built on this tree's own cryptography \(em no host
.IR ssh (1),
no
.B #C
transport. It speaks exactly one of everything the modern OpenSSH
default settled on, the same deliberately small surface
.I ssh.c
carries: the
.B curve25519-sha256
key exchange, a host key that is
.B ssh-rsa
(exchange-hash signature
.BR rsa-sha2-256 ),
.B ecdsa-sha2-nistp256
(verified with
.IR p256 (2)),
or
.B ssh-ed25519
(verified with
.IR ed25519 (2)),
and the
.B chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
authenticated packet cipher. There is no GSSAPI and no compression.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once before anything else; it loads the
supporting modules
.RI ( keyring (2),
.IR pkcs (2)
for the RSA host-key verification, the
.B Random
and
.B Encoding
modules, and the raw ChaCha20 / one-shot Poly1305 keyring primitives
\(em see
.IR chachatest (1)).
.PP
.B Connect
dials
.I addr
(a real dial string such as
.BR tcp!140.82.113.3!22 ,
since dial-by-name is not wired up under hosted
.IR emu ),
runs the SSH version exchange and key exchange against
.IR host ,
and verifies the server's
.B rsa-sha2-256
signature over the exchange hash. It returns only if that signature
checks out, so a returned
.B Conn
has a cryptographically authenticated transcript. On success the
connection is encrypted and the session keys are installed; the caller
then drives userauth and channels over
.B writepkt
and
.BR readpkt .
.PP
.I Host
is used in the exchange hash and in messages.
.I Wantthumb ,
if non-empty, must equal the server's computed
.B "sha256=<base64>"
host-key thumbprint or the connection is refused: this is a
fail-closed check, with no silent trust-on-first-use. The thumbprint
is the OpenSSH
.B SHA256:
fingerprint form (\c
.IR ssh-keyscan "(1) then " ssh-keygen (1)
on a Unix host yield the same value), and is also left in
.B Conn.thumb
for a caller that wants to implement its own known-hosts policy.
.PP
.B Writepkt
frames, pads, encrypts and sends one packet whose
.I payload
begins with the SSH message-type byte.
.B Readpkt
reads one packet, verifies its Poly1305 tag
.I before
decrypting the body, and returns the decrypted payload (message-type
byte first) or an error at end of file or on a bad tag. Both honour
.B Conn.encrypted:
the version exchange and key exchange run in the clear, and the
ciphers switch on together at
.BR MSG_NEWKEYS .
.PP
.B Close
drops the connection.
.SS Authentication and commands
.B Rsakey_pem
parses a PEM PKCS#1 RSA private key \(em the format
.B "ssh-keygen -m PEM"
writes \(em into an
.B Authkey:
the
.B ssh-rsa
public-key blob to present (byte-identical to an
.B authorized_keys
line's base64 body) and the private
.RB ( n , d )
used to sign.
.PP
.B Rsakey_factotum
instead returns an
.B Authkey
backed by
.IR factotum (4):
it opens the agent's rpc, starts
.B "proto=rsa role=client"
(with any extra
.IR keyspec ,
e.g.\&
.BR service=ssh ),
and reads the offered key's modulus and exponent to form the blob. The
private key is never read; signing hands the padded block back to
factotum, which raises it to the private exponent \(em the Plan 9
model. This tree's factotum rsa proto returns both
.I n
and the exponent
.I ek
(matching 9front), which the client needs because
.B auth/rsagen
does not use a fixed exponent.
.PP
.B Login
authenticates to a connected server as
.IR user .
It sends the
.B ssh-userauth
service request, then tries publickey (when
.I key
is non-nil) and password (when
.I password
is non-empty), in that order, returning
.B nil
once the server accepts. The publickey exchange sends the unsigned
probe first, and on
.B PK_OK
signs the session id and request with RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 over SHA-256
(computed directly \(em
.IR pkcs (2)'s
.B rsa_sign
predates rsa-sha2-256 and is not used).
.PP
.B Run
opens a session channel, execs
.IR cmd ,
and reads to the end of the channel, returning a
.B Result
whose
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
hold the two data streams and whose
.B status
is the remote exit status.
.PP
.B Shell
opens an interactive session: it optionally requests a pseudo-terminal
(when
.I usepty
is set, with terminal type
.I term
and window size
.IR cols x rows ),
starts the remote shell, and then shuttles bytes \(em following the
Plan 9 model (\c
.IR telnet (1)),
where the client does no terminal emulation of its own and
.IR vt (1)
is what interprets escape sequences and draws. Two processes run: one
copies
.I in
to the channel, the other copies channel output to
.I out
and standard-error data to
.IR err .
It returns the remote exit status when the channel closes. Local raw
mode (\c
.IR cons (3)'s
.BR /dev/consctl )
is the caller's responsibility \(em
.IR ssh (1)
sets it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/ssh.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sshtest (1),
.IR chachatest (1),
.IR tls12 (2),
.IR pkcs (2),
.IR p256 (2),
.IR ed25519 (2),
.IR keyring (2)
.SH BUGS
Only one algorithm of each kind is offered; a server without
.B curve25519-sha256
or
.B chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
cannot be reached, by design.
.PP
.B Run
is one-shot: it advertises a large receive window and never sends
channel data, so it suits commands whose output is bounded.
.B Shell
handles the interactive case, but relies on
.IR vt (1)
for terminal emulation and does not itself relay window-size changes
mid-session. Key re-exchange during a long session is not implemented.
Host keys may be RSA, ECDSA P-256, or ed25519, but client
authentication is RSA only, whether from a file or from
.IR factotum (4);
ed25519 and ECDSA client keys are not handled.
